\subsection{Phase E: analytical hybrid recovery and non-OU model mismatch}
\label{sec:stability-widening-phase-e}

The hybrid theorem of Sec.~\ref{sec:hybrid-stability} permits a hard reset to
leave the inner Live set provided the jump remains safe and the subsequent flow
returns to that set.  The source tilt relock has additional structure that can
be used to sharpen the generic jump inequality: the reset replaces tilt from
the accelerometer and therefore removes dependence on the pre-reset tilt error.

Let a tilt relock satisfy the physical specific-force bound
\begin{equation}
 -\vct f_T^B=g\vct d+\vct a_T,
 \qquad \|\vct a_T\|\le\bar f_T<g,
 \qquad
 \bar\theta_T:=\sin^{-1}(\bar f_T/g).
 \label{eq:widen-tilt-reset-force}
\end{equation}
By the same tangent-ball argument as Lemma~\ref{lem:widen-global-reset}, the
post-reset tilt obeys
\begin{equation}
 \theta_\perp^+\le\bar\theta_T
 \label{eq:widen-tilt-reset-bound}
\end{equation}
independently of $\theta_\perp^-$.  The preserve-yaw source path leaves the
adopted heading gauge unchanged while the additive navigation coordinates are
not hard-reset.

Let $V_k$ be the Phase-B metric and decompose its pre-jump contribution into the
reset tilt coordinate and all coordinates left physically continuous by the
jump.  Uniform metric equivalence across the bounded covariance reset gives a
finite constant $\kappa_U$ for the unchanged coordinates.  Let $c_T$ map the
post-reset group energy into the dimensionless Lyapunov metric.  Then the reset
has the source-shaped estimate
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 V^+\le \kappa_U V_U^-+\delta_T^{\rm an},
 \qquad
 \delta_T^{\rm an}:=c_T(1-\cos\bar\theta_T)+c_{T,d}\bar d_T^2,}
 \label{eq:widen-reset-to-set}
\end{equation}
where $V_U^-$ excludes the discarded pre-reset tilt energy and $\bar d_T$ covers
bounded discrete reset/model defects.  Unlike a generic
$V^+\le\kappa_TV^-+\delta_T$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-reset-to-set} contains no
multiplicative dependence on arbitrarily large pre-reset tilt.

For the source cooldown count $N_{\rm cool}$, Lemma~\ref{lem:widen-constructive-ues}
and Theorem~\ref{thm:widen-explicit-P} give the homogeneous flow factor
\begin{equation}
 \lambda_{\rm cool}^{\rm an}:=
 p_+^{\rm an}M^2\rho^{2N_{\rm cool}}.
 \label{eq:widen-cooldown-factor}
\end{equation}
If bounded Live inputs contribute $\gamma_{\rm cool}$ over the same interval,
then
\begin{equation}
 V_{j+1}^-
 \le\lambda_{\rm cool}^{\rm an}V_j^+
 +\gamma_{\rm cool}.
 \label{eq:widen-cooldown-flow}
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}[Analytical recurrent tilt-reset small gain]
\label{thm:widen-hybrid-small-gain}
Suppose every tilt jump and cooldown prefix remains in the Phase-C safe
geodesic/correction domain and define
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mu_T^{\rm an}:=\kappa_U\lambda_{\rm cool}^{\rm an}<1.}
 \label{eq:widen-hybrid-mu}
\end{equation}
Then at successive pre-reset sections,
\begin{equation}
 V_{j+1}^-
 \le\mu_T^{\rm an}V_j^-
 +\lambda_{\rm cool}^{\rm an}\delta_T^{\rm an}
 +\gamma_{\rm cool},
 \label{eq:widen-reset-poincare}
\end{equation}
and hence
\begin{equation}
 \limsup_{j\to\infty}V_j^-
 \le
 \frac{\lambda_{\rm cool}^{\rm an}\delta_T^{\rm an}
       +\gamma_{\rm cool}}
      {1-\mu_T^{\rm an}}.
 \label{eq:widen-reset-ultimate}
\end{equation}
The pre-reset tilt magnitude does not appear in the multiplicative loop gain.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Substitute Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-reset-to-set} into
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-cooldown-flow}, use $V_U^-\le V^-$, and iterate the scalar
affine recursion.  The source dwell/cooldown logic already excludes Zeno
behavior, so the section index is well defined.
\end{proof}

Magnetic hard re-gauging is different: it changes the adopted horizontal
coordinate frame rather than the physical tilt.  On the full-heading gauge
coordinates it is a bounded symmetry transformation; on the gravity-only
quotient it is the identity equivalence class.  It therefore consumes only the
bounded gauge-rate/jump input already present in the hybrid theorem and need
not be assigned an artificial attitude-capture radius.

\subsubsection{Bounded departures from the OU latent-acceleration model}

The OU state is a regularizing model, not a claim that physical ocean
acceleration is literally an Ornstein--Uhlenbeck sample path.  Let the physical
latent acceleration satisfy
\begin{equation}
 \dot{\vct a}_w
 =-\tau_m^{-1}\vct a_w+\vct w_{OU}+\vct d_m,
 \label{eq:widen-model-mismatch}
\end{equation}
where $\tau_m$ is the model value used by the filter and $\vct d_m$ collects
bounded non-OU dynamics, unresolved harmonics, hydrodynamic forcing, and model
mismatch.  Over one sample this contributes an additional bounded error input
$\mat G_{m,k}\vct d_{m,k}$ with
$\|\mat G_{m,k}\|\le\bar G_m$ on the compact sample-time envelope.

For a Phase-C tube let $\ell<\ell_*$ be its structured nonlinear load and define
the strict deterministic decrease margin
\begin{equation}
 a_0:=1-2p_+^{\rm an}\bar A\ell-p_+^{\rm an}\ell^2>0.
 \label{eq:widen-a0}
\end{equation}
For a generic bounded additive input with matrix norm $\bar G_d$, Young's
inequality applied to the exact Phase-B metric gives
\begin{align}
 V_{k+1}
 &\le(1-\alpha_d)V_k+c_d\|\vct d_k\|^2,
 \label{eq:widen-deterministic-drift}\\
 \alpha_d&:=\frac{a_0}{2p_+^{\rm an}},\\
 c_d&:=p_+^{\rm an}\bar G_d^2
 +\frac{2[p_+^{\rm an}(\bar A+\ell)\bar G_d]^2}{a_0}.
 \label{eq:widen-deterministic-gains}
\end{align}

\begin{theorem}[Practical ISS under bounded non-OU forcing]
\label{thm:widen-model-mismatch-iss}
If the Phase-C tube is robustly invariant for the declared total input bound,
then Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-deterministic-drift} implies
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 V_k\le(1-\alpha_d)^{k-j}V_j
 +\frac{c_d}{\alpha_d}
  \|\vct d\|_{\infty,[j,k)}^2.}
 \label{eq:widen-model-iss}
\end{equation}
In particular, bounded $\vct d_m$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-model-mismatch}
changes the ultimate error bound but does not destroy practical stability.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The zero-input nonlinear terms leave the margin $a_0\|z_k\|^2$.  The input
cross term is bounded by
$2p_+^{\rm an}(\bar A+\ell)\bar G_d\|z_k\|\|d_k\|$ and the direct quadratic
term by $p_+^{\rm an}\bar G_d^2\|d_k\|^2$.  Young's inequality spends half the
margin on the cross term, yielding Eqs.~\eqref{eq:widen-deterministic-drift}--
\eqref{eq:widen-deterministic-gains}.  Since
$V_k\le p_+^{\rm an}\|z_k\|^2$, the remaining state decrease is at least
$\alpha_dV_k$.  Scalar iteration proves Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-model-iss}.
\end{proof}

A mismatch in correlation time is included explicitly.  If the physical
reference model has $\tau_*$ while the filter uses $\tau_m$, then
\begin{equation}
 -\tau_*^{-1}\vct a_w
 =-\tau_m^{-1}\vct a_w
 +\underbrace{(\tau_m^{-1}-\tau_*^{-1})\vct a_w}_{\vct d_\tau}.
 \label{eq:widen-tau-mismatch}
\end{equation}
Thus whenever $\|\vct a_w\|\le A_w$,
\begin{equation}
 \|\vct d_\tau\|
 \le |\tau_m^{-1}-\tau_*^{-1}|A_w.
 \label{eq:widen-tau-mismatch-bound}
\end{equation}
The same ISS gain therefore quantifies robustness to a bounded correlation-time
error rather than requiring equality between an assumed physical OU time
constant and the adapted filter time constant.

\begin{remark}[Interpretation of the OU assumption]
\label{rem:widen-ou-regularizer}
The stability claim produced by Phase E is consequently a robustness statement:
OU dynamics define the nominal regularizing state transition, while bounded
violations are inputs with explicit gain.  The wave-spectrum and reduced-MSE
arguments used to choose $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ remain performance arguments;
they are not prerequisites for the deterministic stability theorem.
\end{remark}
